;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;
;; Metamodel
;;
;; An RDFS-inspired type system layered on top of Datomic. While Datomic
;; provides a powerful schema for attributes (valueType, cardinality, etc.),
;; it does not natively support the notion of "classes" or typed instances.
;; This metamodel fills that gap.
;;
;; Core Concepts:
;;
;;   Resource  - The root of all things (like rdfs:Resource)
;;   Class     - A type definition that can have instances (like rdfs:Class)
;;   Property  - An attribute with domain and range (like rdf:Property)
;;   List      - An ordered collection via cons cells (like rdf:List)
;;
;; Key Properties:
;;
;;   :dt/type        - Links an instance to its class (like rdf:type)
;;   :dt/subclass-of - Defines class inheritance (like rdfs:subClassOf)
;;   :dt/domain      - Restricts which classes may have a property
;;   :dt/range       - Specifies the type of a property's values
;;   :dt/slots       - Declares which properties belong to a class
;;
;; Integration with Datomic:
;;
;; The metamodel complements rather than replaces Datomic's schema. Each
;; :dt/Property is also a Datomic attribute with :db/valueType and
;; :db/cardinality. Native Datomic attributes like :db/doc and :db/ident
;; are incorporated as properties with appropriate domain/range constraints.
;;
;; This layering enables:
;;   - Class-based typing via :dt/type
;;   - Inheritance hierarchies via :dt/subclass-of
;;   - Slot inheritance (classes inherit parent slots)
;;   - Validation against class definitions
;;   - Introspection of the type system itself
;;
;; The schema is bootstrapped carefully: forward declarations establish
;; idents, then properties are defined, then classes reference those
;; properties. Some entities are defined twice - first as placeholders,
;; then augmented with full metadata.
;;
;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;


